# Vercel Speed Insights Implementation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this React + Vite project.

## What Was Implemented

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` version 2.0.0 using npm
- Updated package.json and package-lock.json with the new dependency

### 2. Speed Insights Integration
- Added the SpeedInsights component import to `src/App.tsx`
- Integrated the `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the main App component
- Placed the component at the root level to track all routes (/, /game, /leaderboard)

### 3. Test Configuration
- Created a Jest mock at `src/__mocks__/@vercel/speed-insights.tsx` to handle ESM module imports in test environment
- Updated Jest configuration in package.json to map `@vercel/speed-insights/react` to the mock
- This ensures all tests pass without requiring the actual Speed Insights component to run during testing

## Files Modified

### Created:
- `src/__mocks__/@vercel/speed-insights.tsx` - Mock for Jest tests

### Modified:
- `src/App.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights component import and integration
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency and Jest moduleNameMapper configuration
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency resolution

## Implementation Details

The implementation follows the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart):

1. The SpeedInsights component is imported from `@vercel/speed-insights/react`
2. The component is placed inside the main App component to track all page views
3. No additional configuration is needed - the component will automatically collect and send performance metrics when deployed to Vercel

## Testing & Verification

✅ Build verification: `npm run build` - Successful
✅ Linter check: `npm run lint` - No new errors (only pre-existing warning in mock file)
✅ Test suite: `npm test` - All 31 tests passing (4 test suites)

## Next Steps

After deploying to Vercel:
1. The SpeedInsights component will automatically inject the tracking script
2. Performance metrics will be collected from real user visits
3. Data will be visible in the Vercel dashboard under Speed Insights
4. The script will appear in the page's head as `/<unique-path>/script.js`

The implementation is production-ready and will activate automatically upon Vercel deployment.
